The Tonga flag geometry yields a matrix whose rows fall into (at most) three distinct combinatorial classes once the canton height, cross thickness, and field are discretized:

- entirely \(a\) (all rows below the canton, or any row whose vertical coordinate intersects the horizontal arm of the cross);
- the narrow-central-red pattern \(b\)-\(a\)-\(b\)-\(a\) (canton rows that intersect only the vertical arm);
- the wide-central-red pattern \(b\)-\(a'\)-\(b\)-\(a\) with \(a'>a\) block length (canton rows that intersect the horizontal arm).

These three vectors are linearly independent for generic scalars \(a\neq b\) (e.g., \(a=0\), \(b=1\)). Consequently the row space has dimension exactly 3 and no finer pixelation can produce additional distinct rows. Hence the maximal attainable rank is 3.

