The four white rectangles arise from an axis-aligned red Greek cross inside the canton. This partitions the canton-height rows into three combinatorial families whose white-column supports differ:

- outside canton height: all-a (W-row identically zero);
- canton height but off the horizontal arm: two symmetric b-blocks whose inner edges are set by the vertical-arm width;
- horizontal-arm rows: two strictly narrower b-blocks (inner edges set by the wider horizontal-arm width).

These three distinct nonzero row vectors of W are linearly independent on any grid resolving the arm widths (different column-support sets cannot be scalar multiples). Hence rank(W) ≤ 2 and, via the decomposition M = aJ + (b-a)W, rank(M) ≤ 3. The bound is attained for generic a ≠ b. All lower estimates rest on the incorrect assertion that the two inner-canton supports coincide after rasterization; they do not.

